 d=\"M10 6a2 2 0 110-4 2 2 0 010 4zM10 12a2 2 0 110-4 2 2 0 010 4zM10 18a2 2 0 110-4 2 2 0 010 4z\"></path></svg></button></div><div data-hf-id=\"hf-b1gq\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__textWrap\"><h1 data-hf-id=\"hf-o7pd\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__title\">Water-cycle presentation</h1><div data-hf-id=\"hf-l57t\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__snippet\" data-layout-allow-overlap=\"\" data-layout-allow-occlusion=\"\">Of course! The water cycle is water’s journey around Earth. Here’s a simple explanation for your presentation:\n<strong data-hf-id=\"hf-4310\">Evaporation:</strong> The sun warms water in rivers, lakes and oceans, turning it into invisible water vapour.\n<strong data-hf-id=\"hf-xi8r\">Condensation:</strong> As the vapour cools, it forms tiny droplets that gather into clouds.\n<strong data-hf-id=\"hf-0wlt\">Precipitation:</strong> Water falls from clouds as rain or snow.\n<strong data-hf-id=\"hf-jskg\">Collection:</strong> Water gathers in rivers, lakes and oceans, while some soaks into the ground. Then the journey begins again!\nYou can remember it as: up, clouds, down, and around!\n<span data-hf-id=\"hf-cbt9\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__markdownLink\">Source: USGS Water Science School</span></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-hf-id=\"hf-9o0a\" class=\"History-module__Y977EG__dayGroup\"><div data-hf-id=\"hf-p6ei\" class=\"History-module__Y977EG__dayHeader\"><h1 data-hf-id=\"hf-tkbr\" class=\"History-module__Y977EG__dayTitle\">2026-10-09</h1></div><div data-hf-id=\"hf-2mmw\" class=\"History-module__Y977EG__chatGroup\"><div data-hf-id=\"hf-nlae\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__root\"><div data-hf-id=\"hf-tzw7\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__menu ChatCard-module__coi--a__menuClosed\" data-chat-options-menu=\"true\" data-chat-options-state=\"closed\" data-pw=\"history-chat-options-menu\"><div data-hf-id=\"hf-16ku\" class=\"ChatCard-module__coi--a__menuInner\"><div data-hf-id=\"hf-wkje\" class=\"